course: biology
lesson: 355
title: Hair: growing, curly and straight, eyebrows, eyelashes and going grey
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Each hair grows from a tiny pocket in the skin called a follicle; only the root down in the follicle is alive, and the strand you see is dead keratin, the same stuff as your nails.
explain: That is why a haircut does not hurt but having your hair pulled does: the scissors cut dead strands, while pulling tugs on a living root full of nerves.
explain: The shape of the follicle decides the shape of the hair: a round one grows straight hair, a flat or oval one grows curly hair, the way a round or a flat nozzle shapes icing.
explain: Hair colour comes from melanin made in the root; when the colour cells slow down and stop, new hairs grow in white, and a head of white and dark hairs mixed looks grey.
fact: A head has about 100,000 hairs; blondes often have more and redheads fewer, and losing about 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal.
fact: Hair grows about one centimetre a month; each hair grows for years, then rests and falls out, and a new one grows from the same follicle.
fact: The longest hair recorded was over five metres, grown by a woman in China.
fact: The hair you see is dead keratin, the same material as nails; only the root under the skin is alive, and shaving does not make hair grow back thicker.
fact: Round follicles grow straight hair and flat or oval follicles grow curly hair, and hair shape is inherited from your family.
fact: Eyebrows guide sweat and rain away from the eyes and help show feelings such as surprise or anger; each has a few hundred hairs.
fact: Each upper eyelid has about 100 to 150 lashes and the lower one fewer; lashes keep dust out and make you blink when something touches them.
fact: Tiny harmless mites live in the roots of the eyelashes of most adults.
fact: Hair colour comes from two kinds of melanin, a brown-black one and a red-yellow one, mixed in different amounts; natural red hair is the rarest, about one or two people in a hundred worldwide; strong summer sun breaks down some of the melanin in hair already grown, so hair often looks lighter in summer.
fact: Grey hair grows when colour cells in the roots stop making melanin, and many people start to go grey in their thirties or forties.
fact: The story of hair turning white overnight from a fright is doubted by scientists, because the hair already grown is dead and cannot change colour from inside.
fact: Fine hair covers almost all the body except the palms, the soles and the lips, and helps you feel touch, like an insect landing; babies before birth are covered in fine hair that is lost before or soon after birth.
fact: Rubbing a balloon on dry hair moves tiny electric charges, and each hair pushes away from the others; this is static, and it works best in dry winter air.
exercise: About how many hairs are on a person's head? | About 100,000 | About 1,000 | About 10 million | About 500
exercise: How many hairs a day is it normal to lose? | About 50 to 100 | None at all | About 10,000 | About one a week
exercise: About how fast does head hair grow? | About one centimetre a month | About one centimetre a day | About one metre a year | About one millimetre a year
exercise: What is the hair you can see made of? | Dead keratin, like nails | Living cells full of nerves | Dried blood | Hardened sweat
exercise: Why does a haircut not hurt? | The hair you cut is dead | The barber uses special scissors | Hair has nerves only at night | The scalp is numb during a haircut
exercise: Does shaving make hair grow back thicker? | No, that is a myth | Yes, twice as thick | Yes, but only on the legs | Yes, and darker too
exercise: What decides whether hair is curly or straight? | The shape of the follicle | How often you wash it with shampoo | How much milk you drink | Sleeping on wet hair every night
exercise: What do eyebrows do for the eyes? | Guide sweat and rain away from them | Help the eyes see things clearly in the dark | Keep the eyelids warm | Hold the eyelashes in place
exercise: What do eyelashes do? | Keep dust out and make you blink when touched | Help your eyes focus on things that are far away | Make tears | Keep the eyeballs warm
exercise: What lives in the eyelash roots of most adults? | Tiny harmless mites | Small worms that eat tears | Head lice | Nothing at all
exercise: Which natural hair colour is the rarest? | Red | Black | Brown | Blond
exercise: Why does hair go grey? | The colour cells in the roots stop making melanin | The hair is slowly bleached by many years of sunshine | The hair dries out with age | Grey dust settles in the hair
exercise: Can a fright turn your hair white overnight? | Scientists doubt it, because the grown hair is dead | Yes, it happens to most people after a really bad fright | Yes, but only to children | Yes, in cold weather
exercise: Where on your body is there no hair at all? | Palms, soles and lips | Arms, legs and the back | Ears and inside the nose | Tummy and neck
exercise: Why does a rubbed balloon make hair stand up? | Static charges make each hair push away from the others | The balloon sucks air out of the hair | The hair is frightened by the noise | The balloon glues the hair to itself
test: Count them: two upper eyelids with about 100 lashes each. About how many upper lashes is that? | About 200 | About 100 | About 50 | About 400
test: If you never cut your hair for a year, about how much longer would it be? | About 12 centimetres | About 1 centimetre | About 1 metre | About 50 centimetres
test: Count them: a boy loses about 100 hairs a day. About how many hairs does he lose in a week? | About 700 | About 100 | About 7 | About 70,000
test: «shaving me legs makes the hair grow back thick and black innit» What is right? | No, it only looks blunt because it is cut straight across | Yes, every single shave makes it grow back twice as thick | Yes, but only in summer | No, shaved hair never grows back
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom sees a ghost and his fur turns white in a second. What is the truth? | The fur already grown is dead and cannot change colour from a fright | Fright really bleaches fur instantly | Only cats can turn white from fright | It happens if the fright is long enough
test: «pulling my hair hurts but the hairdresser cutting it don't, that's weird» Why? | Pulling tugs the living root with nerves; cutting only cuts dead hair | The hairdresser numbs the hair first with a special cream that stops the pain | Hair has nerves only at the tips | Pulling hair breaks bones
test: A girl with straight hair asks why her friend's is curly. What is the best answer? | Her friend's follicles are flat or oval, a shape inherited from her family | Her friend sleeps with wet hair | Her friend eats more crusts | Her friend's hair is older
test: Why does hair often get lighter in summer? | Sunlight breaks down some of the melanin in the dead hair | New hair grows in white because of the heat | Summer air bleaches the roots | Sweat dyes it lighter
test: «Grandad's grey cos his hair's dusty from the shed» What is right? | Grey hair grows when the root's colour cells stop making melanin | Yes, the dust from the shed slowly gets stuck in his hair and makes it grey | Grey hair is dead and the dark hair is alive | Grey comes from not washing enough
test: What would happen to sweat on your forehead if you had no eyebrows? | More would run straight into your eyes | It would stop sweating | It would turn to steam | It would run up into your hair
test: Why does a balloon trick work best on a dry winter day? | Dry air lets static charges build up instead of leaking away | Cold air makes each hair lighter, so it lifts up more easily | Winter hair is longer | Balloons are bigger in winter
exam: Identify the material of which the visible portion of a hair is composed. | Keratin, which is dead tissue | Living tissue supplied with nerves | Hardened perspiration | Dried blood
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determinant of the curliness of hair? | The shape of the follicle, which is inherited | The frequency with which the hair is washed | The quantity of milk consumed in childhood | The practice of sleeping with damp hair
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of shaving upon the subsequent growth of hair. | Shaving does not render the hair thicker | Shaving doubles the thickness of the hair | Shaving darkens the hair permanently | Shaving terminates the growth of hair
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the greying of hair. | The cessation of the production of melanin by pigment cells in the roots | The bleaching of the hair by sunlight over decades | The accumulation of dust upon the hair | The drying of the hair in consequence of age
